---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Building Caderno UI"
  description="From a product's notebook interface to a shared component library."
>
  <header class="page-header">
    <h1>Building Caderno UI</h1><p>
      A shared library for interfaces that feel like personal notes on white
      paper.
    </p>
  </header>
  <section class="content-section">
    <h2>The problem</h2><p>
      SeniorPath needed a consistent reading and annotation interface. Keeping a
      separate catalog and local component styles in the app made changes
      diverge.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="content-section">
    <h2>The decisions</h2><ul>
      <li>
        Keep behavior and styling in Lit custom elements. React and Astro
        translate framework contracts; they do not duplicate components.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use slots for product content and consumer-owned navigation. A list item
        should not decide which router an app uses.
      </li>
      <li>
        Share white surfaces, blue ink, handwriting and border tokens. Preserve
        readable native content before JavaScript.
      </li>
      <li>
        Offer selective imports. Charts and motion are optional dependencies of
        the experience, not every button.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="content-section">
    <h2>Use and evidence</h2><p>
      SeniorPath is the real Astro consumer. This documentation also uses the
      library. A <a href={`${base}/examples/react/`}
        >small React / Next.js notes app</a
      > exercises forms, navigation, confirmation and feedback.
    </p><p>
      <a href={`${base}/quality/`}
        >See the validation scope and remaining limits</a
      >. Package, browser and integration checks run before publication.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
